-- | Ipe.PubSub — Task-shaped publish, callable wherever a bus runs.
--
-- A complement to `Ipe.Tea.Web.PubSub.publish` (the Cmd form, fired
-- from a Ipe.Web `update` return). `Ipe.PubSub.publish` returns a
-- `Task Error Int` you can run from raw `Ipe.Http.Server` `api`
-- handlers, post-init tasks, scheduled jobs, callbacks from
-- external systems — anywhere you hold a `Task` and would otherwise
-- have no `Cmd msg` channel to use. The broadcast bus lives in the
-- running Web/live runtime, so the Task succeeds while a `Web.app`
-- is serving in this process and resolves to `Err` otherwise.
--
-- The Task resolves with the count of subscribers that received
-- the broadcast (the same `Int` `app.Publish` returns inside the
-- update-loop path), or `Err Unavailable` when no `Web.app` is
-- running in this process (a CLI tool, an isolated unit test, a
-- pure `Ipe.Http.Server` process).
--
-- Scope (matches `Ipe.Tea.Web.PubSub.publish`): in-process only.
-- The same topic registry backs both APIs, so a publish from a
-- raw handler reaches every subscribed Ipe.Web session in the
-- same process, with identical ordering + echo semantics.
--
-- See `examples/shapes/web/task-publish` for the canonical pattern.
--
-- The pub/sub fan-out comes in two flavours:
--   * `publish`        — echo-by-default. Server-side publishes
--                        carry an empty origin, so a Task-shaped
--                        caller's own subscription (if any) is not
--                        suppressed.
--   * `publishNoEcho`  — sets the broker's skip-origin bit, so the
--                        publishing session's own subscription is
--                        suppressed. For a Task-shaped caller with
--                        an empty origin this is a no-op.
module Ipe.PubSub exposing (Topic, topic, publish, publishNoEcho)

import Ipe.Ffi.Kernel as Kernel
import Ipe.Error exposing (Error)
import Ipe.Task as Task


-- | Phantom topic handle.  The type parameter `a` is the payload type shared
-- between publisher and subscriber — the compiler enforces that both sides
-- agree on `a` at type-check time.  At runtime a `Topic a` erases to the
-- bare topic-name string; there is no allocation or wrapper.
--
-- Construct with `PubSub.topic`; never construct `Topic` directly — the
-- single opaque constructor exists only to make this a valid type declaration.
type Topic a
    = Topic


-- | `topic name` — create a typed topic handle from a plain topic-name string.
--
-- The resulting `Topic a` carries `a` only at type-check time; at runtime it
-- is the bare name string.  Sharing the same `Topic a` value between `publish`
-- and `Sub.subscribeTopic` (or `Cmd.publish`) guarantees they agree on the
-- payload type `a`.
--
--   chatTopic : Topic (Dict String String)
--   chatTopic = PubSub.topic "chat"
topic : String -> Topic a
topic =
    Kernel.kernel "PubSub_topic"


-- | `publish t payload` — broadcast `payload` on topic `t` to every Ipe.Web
-- session in this process whose `subscriptions` includes
-- `Sub.subscribeTopic t _`.
--
-- The type parameter `a` must match the subscriber's handler type; mismatches
-- are caught at compile time via the shared `Topic a` handle.
--
-- Returns the count of subscribers that received the broadcast.
-- A topic with no live subscribers returns 0 (not an error).
--
-- Fire-and-forget by design — no acknowledgement loop, no
-- delivery guarantee beyond the in-process broker's invariant
-- (one bump per publish, before fan-out). Pair with a durable
-- write (DB row, append-only log) when persistence matters.
--
-- Errors with `Unavailable` when no `Web.app` is registered in
-- this process. CLI tools / pure HTTP servers / unit tests that
-- never start a `Web.app` see this; production deploys never
-- should.
publish : Topic a -> a -> Task Error Int
publish =
    Kernel.kernel "PubSub_publish"


-- | `publishNoEcho t payload` — Task-shaped publish that sets the broker's
-- skip-origin bit on the outgoing event, so the publishing session's own
-- subscription is suppressed.
--
-- Called server-side the difference is cosmetic: the origin is empty, so no
-- subscriber's own-sid matches it and nothing is suppressed.  It differs from
-- `publish` only when the caller shares an origin with one of the
-- subscriptions.
--
-- Same `Err` semantics as `publish` when no `Web.app` is running in this
-- process.
publishNoEcho : Topic a -> a -> Task Error Int
publishNoEcho =
    Kernel.kernel "PubSub_publishNoEcho"
